Run once on an XAUUSD M5 chart with enough history loaded, this is exactly what produced the 500-bar export that Fig. 2 and Fig. 3 are calibrated from and walked through. It writes to the shared
Common\\Files
folder, the same convention the EA's own diagnostics log uses, so the export survives terminal reinstalls and any offline script can find it without guessing which terminal instance produced it.
Basis Functions and the Native OLS Solver
The regression basis is what the continuation-value estimate depends on. Too few terms and the fit can't capture the decision boundary's shape; too many and it overfits the modest in-the-money sample at early steps. Six terms strikes that balance: an intercept, the payoff, its square (curvature near the boundary, as in the original Longstaff-Schwartz basis), the fraction of horizon elapsed, the best payoff seen so far, and a payoff/time interaction
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| BuildBasisRow                                                    |
//| Basis functions at one path/time step, matching SolveOLS's       |
//|   fixed X[][6] layout: 1.0, pnl_now, pnl_now^2 (curvature, as in |
//|   the original LS basis), time_frac, peak_pnl (best payoff so    |
//|   far), and pnl_now*time_frac (payoff/time interaction).         |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
BuildBasisRow(
const
double
pnl_now,
const
double
peak_pnl,
const
double
time_frac,
double
&row[])
const
{
ArrayResize
(row,
6
);
      row[
0
] =
1.0
;
      row[
1
] = pnl_now;
      row[
2
] = pnl_now * pnl_now;
      row[
3
] = time_frac;
      row[
4
] = peak_pnl;
      row[
5
] = pnl_now * time_frac;
     }
The fourth term - the running peak of the payoff along a path - is a cheap stand-in for a trailing or drawdown-from-peak effect, without carrying the full path history as a state variable. A path that ran up to +40 points and has since faded to +10 is a meaningfully different state from one that has only ever been at +10, even though the instantaneous payoff is identical, and this term lets the regression tell the two apart.
Worth being clear about: this peak-tracking only exists inside each simulated future path. The real position's own trading history - whatever maximum unrealized profit it already reached - is not an input to
EvaluateExit()
at all; the comparison at step zero never sees the real trade's own trailing high, only the raw current exercise payoff against the Monte Carlo average.
Fitting those six coefficients at every backward step is a small least squares problem, solved with a hand-rolled, ridge-regularized Gauss-Jordan elimination rather than any external library. The design matrix is built from the basis rows of the in-the-money paths, and the target vector is each path's discounted realized cash flow
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| SolveOLS                                                         |
//| Fits beta in y ~ X*beta via ridge-regularized Gauss-Jordan:      |
//|   (X'X + lambda*I)*beta = X'y. Returns false (caller falls back  |
//|   to "always continue") if the system couldn't be pivoted.       |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
SolveOLS(
const
double
&X[][
6
],
const
double
&y[],
const
int
rows,
const
int
cols,
const
double
lambda,
double
&beta[])
  {
//--- guard: require at least as many in-the-money paths as basis
//--- terms. Ridge regularization can, in principle, still produce a
//--- solution with fewer rows than columns, so this is a deliberate
//--- reliability choice rather than a mathematical necessity - a fit
//--- built from fewer observations than free parameters is not
//--- trusted to drive a real exit decision here, whatever it solves to
if
(rows < cols)
return
(
false
);
//--- build the symmetric cols x cols Gram matrix XtX and the
//--- cols-length vector Xty by direct summation; this is the
//--- classic normal-equation formulation, cheap here because cols
//--- is fixed and small (the basis has at most 6 terms)
double
xtx[
6
][
6
];
double
xty[
6
];
for
(
int
a =
0
; a < cols; a++)
     {
      xty[a] =
0.0
;
for
(
int
b =
0
; b < cols; b++)
         xtx[a][b] =
0.0
;
     }
for
(
int
r =
0
; r < rows; r++)
     {
for
(
int
a =
0
; a < cols; a++)
        {
//--- accumulate X'y for this row's contribution
xty[a] += X[r][a] * y[r];
for
(
int
b =
0
; b < cols; b++)
            xtx[a][b] += X[r][a] * X[r][b];
        }
     }
//--- add the ridge penalty to the diagonal only; this shrinks the
//--- coefficients toward zero and, critically, guarantees a
//--- strictly positive diagonal even when a basis column is nearly
//--- collinear with another (e.g. pnl and pnl^2 on a narrow path
//--- sample), which is what actually causes Gauss-Jordan to fail
for
(
int
a =
0
; a < cols; a++)
      xtx[a][a] += lambda;
The solver builds the Gram matrix X'X and the vector X'y from direct summation, adds the ridge penalty to the diagonal only, then eliminates with partial pivoting - swapping in whichever remaining row has the largest absolute entry in the pivot column, so the elimination never divides by something close to zero
double
eps =
1.0
e-
12
;
for
(
int
col =
0
; col < cols; col++)
     {
int
pivot_row = col;
double
pivot_val =
MathAbs
(aug[col][col]);
for
(
int
r = col +
1
; r < cols; r++)
        {
if
(
MathAbs
(aug[r][col]) > pivot_val)
           {
            pivot_val =
MathAbs
(aug[r][col]);
            pivot_row = r;
           }
        }
if
(pivot_val < eps)
return
(
false
);
//--- system too degenerate to trust
if
(pivot_row != col)
        {
for
(
int
c =
0
; c <= cols; c++)
           {
double
tmp        = aug[col][c];
            aug[col][c]       = aug[pivot_row][c];
            aug[pivot_row][c] = tmp;
           }
        }
//--- normalize the pivot row so aug[col][col] becomes 1.0
double
piv = aug[col][col];
for
(
int
c =
0
; c <= cols; c++)
         aug[col][c] /= piv;
//--- eliminate this column from every other row (full
//--- Gauss-Jordan, not just below the pivot) so the final
//--- augmented column is the solution directly, with no
//--- separate back-substitution pass required
for
(
int
r =
0
; r < cols; r++)
        {
if
(r == col)
continue
;
double
factor = aug[r][col];
for
(
int
c =
0
; c <= cols; c++)
            aug[r][c] -= factor * aug[col][c];
        }
     }
Two failure modes are handled explicitly rather than left to produce garbage silently. If there are fewer in-the-money paths than basis terms,
SolveOLS()
refuses outright - a deliberate reliability choice, since a fit built from fewer observations than free parameters is not trusted to drive a real exit decision. If every candidate pivot in a column is smaller than 1.0e-12, the function also returns false, signalling a degenerate fit. In both cases
EvaluateExit()
simply skips updating the cash flows for that step, equivalent to assuming "continue" was optimal there - cautious in that it never lets an unreliable regression invent an exercise decision, but not the safer outcome for a currently losing position, which risks letting the loss run rather than cutting it. "Do not trust a degenerate fit" is a more accurate description of this fallback than "the conservative choice."
Two further limitations: the six basis terms aren't scaled to comparable magnitudes before the ridge penalty applies, so a fixed
lambda
doesn't shrink every coefficient equally. And forming the normal equations X'X is numerically harder than solving via QR or SVD, since squaring the design matrix roughly squares its condition number - ridge keeps Gauss-Jordan from failing outright, but doesn't make this as stable as a factorization-based solver.
Assembling the CLSMExitEngine Class
With the simulation, basis, and solver pieces in place, the rest of the class is mostly plumbing: a payoff function, a constructor with safe defaults, and a configuration method turning a nominal annual discount rate into the per-bar discount factor the backward induction uses
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| ExercisePayoff                                                   |
//| Payoff of closing now at 'price', net of an estimated            |
//|   round-trip cost. Direction-aware: a long profits as price      |
//|   rises above entry, a short as it falls below entry.            |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
ExercisePayoff(
const
double
price,
const
double
entry_price,
const
long
pos_type,
const
double
cost_points)
const
{
double
raw = (pos_type ==
POSITION_TYPE_BUY
)
                   ? (price - entry_price)
                   : (entry_price - price);
return
(raw - cost_points);
     }
Keeping this single, small function as the only place that computes a payoff means the simulation, the regression targets, and the live exercise decision all use an identical definition of profit - no rounding or sign inconsistency can creep in between them.
One simplification worth noting:
current_price
is already the bid for a long or ask for a short, so spread is partly embedded in the payoff before
cost_points
is even subtracted. If
cost_points
also includes the full spread, part is double-counted -
